(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-09"] = {"chem-11-09-b01":{"idea":"The Wurtz reaction joins two alkyl groups with sodium to make a longer alkane.","steps":["Two molecules of R–X react with sodium metal.","The two R groups join by a new C–C bond: 2R–X + 2Na → R–R + 2NaX.","The product has twice the carbons of R, and it is an alkane."],"others":"Aromatic hydrocarbons are not made by cyclising halides this way. Alkenes come from dehydrohalogenation. Alkynes come from calcium carbide or from elimination reactions.","answer":"Alkanes, by coupling two alkyl halides"},"chem-11-09-b02":{"idea":"Dehydration of an alcohol removes water to make a double bond.","steps":["Concentrated H₂SO₄ and heat act on the alcohol.","The –OH leaves from one carbon, and an H leaves from the next carbon.","Together they leave as H₂O, and a C=C bond forms."],"others":"No hydrogen gas is lost. The oxygen cannot leave alone as O₂. H₂SO₄ is only a catalyst and is not removed from the alcohol as the product.","answer":"Water: OH from one carbon and H from the next"},"chem-11-09-b03":{"idea":"Calcium carbide gives acetylene when it meets water.","steps":["CaC₂ contains the C₂²⁻ ion.","Water gives H⁺ to this ion and forms C₂H₂.","The equation is CaC₂ + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + C₂H₂."],"others":"Sodium, methane and hydrogen do not react with CaC₂ in these ways. The other equations are not real reactions.","answer":"Water: CaC2 + 2H2O -> Ca(OH)2 + C2H2"},"chem-11-09-b04":{"idea":"Markovnikov's rule tells which carbon gets the halogen.","steps":["When HX adds to an unsymmetrical alkene, H goes to the carbon with more H atoms.","X goes to the carbon with fewer H atoms, the more substituted carbon.","This gives the more stable carbocation."],"others":"X on the less substituted carbon is the anti-Markovnikov product. The two products do not form in equal amounts. X does not always go to the terminal carbon.","trap":"Rich get richer: H goes to the carbon that already has more H.","answer":"X on the more substituted carbon, the one that had fewer H atoms"},"chem-11-09-b05":{"idea":"Benzene is the simplest aromatic hydrocarbon.","steps":["It has a ring of 6 carbon atoms.","Each carbon is bonded to one hydrogen.","So the formula is C₆H₆."],"others":"C₆H₁₂ is cyclohexane. C₆H₁₀ is cyclohexene. C₅H₆ has the wrong number of carbons.","answer":"C6H6"},"chem-11-09-b06":{"idea":"You must pick the statement that is NOT an aromaticity criterion.","steps":["Aromatic rings are cyclic and planar.","Every ring atom takes part in the conjugated pi system.","The ring has 4n + 2 pi electrons (Huckel's rule).","An sp³ carbon breaks the conjugation, so needing one is false."],"others":"The other three statements are real criteria: 4n + 2 pi electrons, cyclic and planar, and full conjugation.","trap":"Read the word NOT.","answer":"The ring must contain at least one sp3-hybridized carbon"},"chem-11-09-b07":{"idea":"Hydrocarbons contain only C and H, so their complete burning has simple products.","steps":["With enough oxygen, carbon becomes CO₂.","Hydrogen becomes H₂O.","So the products are CO₂ and H₂O."],"others":"CO and H₂ appear with too little oxygen. Soot appears with too little oxygen. CO₂ with no water is wrong since the hydrogen has to go somewhere.","answer":"CO2 and H2O"},"chem-11-09-b08":{"idea":"A terminal alkyne has an H on a carbon with triple bond, and that H is weakly acidic.","steps":["The terminal carbon is sp hybridised, which means 50% s-character.","s orbitals are closer to the nucleus, so the carbon holds the bonding electrons tighter.","The C–H bond loses H⁺ more easily than in alkenes or alkanes.","The anion left behind is more stable."],"others":"A methyl group pushes electrons rather than pulling them. The terminal carbon is sp, not sp³. The triple bond does not push electrons onto the C–H.","answer":"The sp carbon has 50% s-character, holding the C-H electrons closer, so H+ leaves more easily"},"chem-11-09-b09":{"idea":"Benzene is very stable because its pi electrons are spread over the whole ring.","steps":["Addition would break the ring's delocalised pi system.","That would cost a lot of stability.","In substitution, one H is replaced and the ring stays intact.","So benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution."],"others":"It does react, so not inert. It does not behave like an alkene. It is not limited to reduction.","answer":"Electrophilic substitution reactions, rather than addition"},"chem-11-09-b10":{"idea":"Electron-donating groups make the ring richer in electrons at certain positions.","steps":["Groups like –OH and –CH₃ push electrons into the ring.","The extra charge sits on the ortho and para carbons.","An electrophile attacks there, so these groups are ortho/para directors and activating."],"others":"–NO₂ is deactivating and meta-directing. Halogens are ortho/para directors, though deactivating. A negative formal charge alone does not decide direction.","answer":"An activating group such as -OH or -CH3"},"chem-11-09-b11":{"idea":"Ozonolysis cuts the double bond completely.","steps":["Ozone adds to C=C and breaks it.","With a reductive work-up, each former alkene carbon becomes C=O.","This gives an aldehyde or a ketone, depending on the substituents."],"others":"Ozone does not add OH groups. An epoxide is made by a different reagent. Carboxylic acid forms only with oxidative work-up, not in every case.","answer":"A carbonyl group (C=O): an aldehyde or ketone, depending on substitution"},"chem-11-09-b12":{"idea":"A radical chain has three stages: initiation, propagation and termination.","steps":["Initiation makes the first radicals.","UV light splits Cl–Cl into two Cl· atoms: Cl₂ → 2Cl·.","This is homolytic cleavage."],"others":"Forming the C–Cl bond happens in propagation. CH₄ does not react directly with HCl. Two Cl radicals joining is termination.","answer":"Homolytic cleavage of Cl2 into two Cl radicals, Cl2 -> 2Cl."},"chem-11-09-b13":{"idea":"Each C=C in the Kekulé drawing provides 2 pi electrons.","steps":["Benzene has 6 carbons, each contributing one p electron.","So there are 6 pi electrons.","They are delocalised over the ring. 6 = 4n + 2 with n = 1."],"others":"12 and 3 do not match 6 p electrons. 4 pi electrons would be anti-aromatic.","answer":"6"},"chem-11-09-b14":{"idea":"Count rings and pi bonds using the DoU formula.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) ÷ 2.","For C₄H₆: (2 × 4 + 2 − 6) ÷ 2.","= (10 − 6) ÷ 2 = 2.","A triple bond counts as 2, which agrees with but-1-yne."],"answer":"2"},"chem-11-09-b15":{"idea":"Balance a combustion equation by counting oxygen atoms.","steps":["Products: 1 CO₂ has 2 O. 2 H₂O have 2 O. Total 4 O atoms.","Each O₂ gives 2 O atoms, so 4 ÷ 2 = 2 mol O₂.","Check: O₂ needed = x + y/4 = 1 + 1 = 2."],"answer":"2 mol"},"chem-11-09-b16":{"idea":"Under normal conditions HBr adds by Markovnikov's rule.","steps":["H⁺ adds to the CH₂ end, which has more H.","This forms a secondary carbocation on the middle carbon.","Br⁻ attaches to the middle carbon.","The major product is 2-bromopropane."],"others":"1,2-Dibromopropane would need Br₂. Propane would need H₂. 1-Bromopropane is the anti-Markovnikov product, formed only with peroxides.","answer":"2-bromopropane (CH3-CHBr-CH3)"},"chem-11-09-b17":{"idea":"Peroxides switch the mechanism to a free-radical chain.","steps":["Peroxide makes Br· radicals from HBr.","Br· adds first to the CH₂ end, giving the more stable secondary radical.","This radical takes H from HBr and the chain continues.","So Br ends on the end carbon: 1-bromopropane."],"others":"2-Bromopropane is the normal Markovnikov product. Peroxides do not stop the reaction. 1,2-Dibromopropane would need Br₂.","trap":"Peroxide gives anti-Markovnikov, with HBr only.","answer":"1-bromopropane (CH3-CH2-CH2Br) -- anti-Markovnikov"},"chem-11-09-b18":{"idea":"If both alkene carbons are alike, there is only one place for Br.","steps":["In CH₃–CH=CH–CH₃ both double-bond carbons have one H and one CH₃.","So H and Br add the same way whichever carbon is chosen.","The product is 2-bromobutane, with or without peroxide."],"others":"1-Bromobutane would need Br on a chain end, which is impossible here. The reaction does occur. The peroxide effect does not matter since there is only one product.","answer":"2-bromobutane -- the SAME product either way, since both alkene carbons are equally substituted"},"chem-11-09-b19":{"idea":"Break the C=C bond and put C=O on each carbon.","steps":["CH₃–CH=CH–CH₃ splits in the middle.","Each half is CH₃–CH, which becomes CH₃–CHO.","Both halves are the same, so you get 2 mol of acetaldehyde."],"others":"Formaldehyde with acetone would come from a different alkene. Acetic acid needs oxidative work-up. Symmetric alkenes do cleave.","answer":"2 mol of acetaldehyde (CH3CHO) -- the same carbonyl compound from both halves"},"chem-11-09-b20":{"idea":"An unsymmetrical alkene gives two different carbonyl compounds.","steps":["CH₃–CH=CH₂ splits between C2 and C3.","The CH₃–CH half becomes CH₃CHO.","The CH₂ half becomes HCHO.","So the products are acetaldehyde and formaldehyde."],"others":"Two moles of CH₃CHO would need a symmetric alkene. Acetic acid and methanol do not form by ozonolysis. Acetone would need a carbon with two alkyl groups.","answer":"1 mol CH3CHO (acetaldehyde) and 1 mol HCHO (formaldehyde)"},"chem-11-09-b21":{"idea":"Check pi electrons against 4n + 2 for aromatic, 4n for anti-aromatic.","steps":["Cyclobutadiene has 4 pi electrons.","4n + 2 gives 2, 6, 10 and so on, so 4 is not allowed.","4 = 4n with n = 1, so it is anti-aromatic.","n must be a whole number, so 0.5 is not valid."],"others":"Not every conjugated ring is aromatic. Four electrons do not meet the aromatic count. n cannot be 0.5.","answer":"No: 4 pi electrons is 4n (n=1), not 4n+2, so it is anti-aromatic"},"chem-11-09-b22":{"idea":"The methyl group donates electrons and activates the ring.","steps":["–CH₃ is an activating, ortho/para-directing group.","The ortho and para positions become richest in electrons.","So nitration gives a mixture of ortho- and para-nitrotoluene."],"others":"The ring is activated, not deactivated, so a reaction occurs. Meta is not the major product. The products are not equal; ortho and para dominate.","answer":"A mixture of ortho- and para-nitrotoluene"},"chem-11-09-b23":{"idea":"The nitro group withdraws electrons and directs the new group meta.","steps":["–NO₂ pulls electrons by –I and –M effects.","The ortho and para positions become the poorest in electrons.","The meta position is least deactivated, so the new group goes there.","The product is meta-dinitrobenzene."],"others":"Ortho and para are the most deactivated here. The mixture is not equal.","answer":"Meta-dinitrobenzene"},"chem-11-09-b24":{"idea":"Friedel–Crafts alkylation needs a Lewis acid to make an electrophile.","steps":["AlCl₃ takes Cl⁻ from CH₃Cl.","This makes a carbocation-like electrophile, CH₃⁺.","The electrophile attacks benzene.","The AlCl₃ must be anhydrous, since water destroys it."],"others":"Aqueous AlCl₃ is not useful because water reacts with it. NaOH is a base. HCl does not make the electrophile.","answer":"Anhydrous AlCl3, a Lewis acid"},"chem-11-09-b25":{"idea":"Balance O atoms to find the oxygen needed.","steps":["Products: 2 CO₂ have 4 O. 3 H₂O have 3 O. Total 7 O atoms.","Each O₂ gives 2 O atoms, so 7 ÷ 2 = 3.5 mol.","Check: x + y/4 = 2 + 6/4 = 3.5."],"answer":"3.5 mol"},"chem-11-09-b26":{"idea":"Balance O atoms for propane.","steps":["Products: 3 CO₂ have 6 O. 4 H₂O have 4 O. Total 10 O atoms.","10 ÷ 2 = 5 mol O₂.","Check: x + y/4 = 3 + 8/4 = 5."],"answer":"5 mol"},"chem-11-09-b27":{"idea":"In a Wurtz reaction, one halide gives R–R, which has an even number of carbons.","steps":["Two identical R groups join, so the carbon count is twice that in R.","That is always even.","Using two different halides R–X and R′–X gives R–R, R–R′ and R′–R′.","A mixture of three alkanes is hard to separate."],"others":"The cost of the second halide is not the reason. Alkyl halides with Na do couple. Odd-carbon halides also react, so the even-carbon claim is wrong.","answer":"One R-X couples to R-R, always an even carbon count; two different halides give a mixture of three alkanes"},"chem-11-09-b28":{"idea":"Terminal alkynes have an acidic H that metals can replace.","steps":["The sp C–H is weakly acidic.","Ag⁺ or Cu⁺ replaces this H and forms a metal acetylide.","The acetylide is an insoluble precipitate.","So the test detects a terminal C≡C–H."],"others":"Aromatic rings do not give this test. Internal alkynes like but-2-yne have no acidic H. Alkenes do not give a precipitate.","answer":"An acidic terminal C-H, replaced by the metal to form a metal acetylide"},"chem-11-09-b29":{"idea":"In catalytic hydrogenation, the alkene sits on the metal surface and takes both H atoms from one side.","steps":["H₂ is adsorbed on Ni, Pd or Pt and split into H atoms.","The alkene lies flat on the surface.","Both H atoms add to the same face.","This is syn addition."],"others":"It does not go through a free carbocation. It adds two H atoms, not one. Anti addition would put the H atoms on opposite faces.","answer":"Syn addition: both H atoms add to the same face from the catalyst surface"},"chem-11-09-b30":{"idea":"Count the pi bonds, because each takes up one H₂.","steps":["A triple bond has 2 pi bonds.","First H₂ gives an alkene.","Second H₂ turns the alkene into an alkane.","So 2 mol H₂ are needed per mol of alkyne."],"answer":"2 mol"},"chem-11-09-b31":{"idea":"Delocalisation lowers the energy of benzene.","steps":["The 6 pi electrons spread over all six carbons.","This makes benzene far more stable than three separate C=C bonds.","Addition would destroy the delocalised ring.","So benzene prefers substitution."],"others":"The C–C bonds of benzene are all equal in length and are not simply stronger C=C bonds. The ring hydrogens do not block the faces of the ring. The carbons are sp², not sp³.","answer":"Delocalisation: the six pi electrons spread over the whole ring, making benzene more stable than three separate C=C bonds; addition would destroy that"},"chem-11-09-b32":{"idea":"Use the DoU formula for C₅H₈.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) ÷ 2.","For C₅H₈: (2 × 5 + 2 − 8) ÷ 2.","= (12 − 8) ÷ 2 = 2.","A triple bond gives 2, which agrees with pent-1-yne."],"answer":"2"},"chem-11-09-b33":{"idea":"Ozonolysis cuts at the double bond, so the fragments show where it was.","steps":["Ozone cleaves C=C and turns each carbon into C=O.","Name the two carbonyl fragments.","Join them back at the C=O carbons.","This shows the position of the double bond."],"others":"The amount of ozone does not give molar mass. Ozonolysis does not distinguish cis from trans. It does not make aromatic compounds.","answer":"Locate exactly where the C=C bond was in the original alkene"},"chem-11-09-b34":{"idea":"A radical chain stops when radicals meet each other.","steps":["Termination happens when two radicals combine, for example Cl· + Cl· → Cl₂.","This removes the chain carriers.","So each chain has a limited length."],"others":"CH₃Cl is not inert, and it can react further. UV light can start many chains, not just one. A radical is regenerated in propagation, not used up.","answer":"Termination: two radicals combine (e.g. 2Cl. -> Cl2), removing the chain carriers"},"chem-11-09-b35":{"idea":"The more substituted carbocation forms, and Br attaches to it.","steps":["(CH₃)₂C=CH₂ gets H⁺ on the CH₂ end.","The cation formed is (CH₃)₃C⁺, which is tertiary.","Br⁻ adds to the tertiary carbon.","The product is 2-bromo-2-methylpropane."],"others":"2-Bromobutane has a different carbon skeleton. 1-Bromo-2-methylpropane is the anti-Markovnikov product. 1,2-Dibromo-2-methylpropane needs Br₂.","answer":"2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)"},"chem-11-09-b36":{"idea":"Lindlar's catalyst is poisoned so that it stops at the alkene.","steps":["Both H atoms add from the same face of the triple bond.","This is syn addition.","Syn addition gives the cis (Z) alkene.","The poison stops the reaction before it reaches the alkane."],"others":"The reaction does not stop at the alkyne. Trans comes from anti addition. The product is not an equal mix.","answer":"Cis (Z) alkene, from syn addition of both H atoms"},"chem-11-09-b37":{"idea":"Na in liquid NH₃ works through a radical anion and gives the more stable alkene.","steps":["The two H atoms add on opposite faces.","This is anti addition.","The product is the trans (E) alkene.","Lindlar's catalyst gives cis, which is the opposite."],"others":"Cis is the Lindlar product. The product is not a 50:50 mix. The reaction stops at the alkene.","answer":"Trans (E) alkene, by anti addition via a radical-anion route"},"chem-11-09-b38":{"idea":"Use O₂ needed = x + y/4 for CₓHᵧ.","steps":["For an alkane, y = 2x + 2.","O₂ = x + (2x + 2) ÷ 4 = 1.5x + 0.5.","A larger x gives a larger O₂ demand.","Check: methane needs 2, ethane 3.5, propane 5."],"others":"It does not decrease or stay constant, since the sequence 2, 3.5, 5 rises. It is related to the carbon count.","answer":"Increases"},"chem-11-09-b39":{"idea":"Sulfonation is reversible, which is unusual among aromatic substitutions.","steps":["Benzene reacts with fuming H₂SO₄ to give benzenesulfonic acid.","Heating with dilute acid or steam removes –SO₃H.","Benzene is formed back.","So sulfonation is reversible."],"others":"SO₃ is an electrophile, not a nucleophile. AlCl₃ is not needed for sulfonation. The C–S bond can be broken, so it is not irreversible.","answer":"Reversible: heating with steam or dilute acid gives back benzene"},"chem-11-09-b40":{"idea":"Alkynes are less reactive to electrophiles because of their sp carbons.","steps":["An sp carbon has 50% s-character, compared with 33% for sp².","s orbitals are nearer the nucleus.","The pi electrons are held more tightly.","So they are less available for an electrophile."],"others":"Alkynes are not aromatic. They have more pi electrons, not fewer. s-character holds electrons closer, not further out.","answer":"sp carbons (more s-character) hold the pi electrons closer, so electrophiles attack them less easily"},"chem-11-09-b41":{"idea":"Use moles to find the CO₂ mass.","steps":["Moles of C₃H₈ = 44 ÷ 44 = 1 mol.","The equation gives 3 mol CO₂ per mol C₃H₈.","Mass of CO₂ = 3 × 44 = 132.0 g.","Check: reactants 44 + 160 = 204 g. Products 132 + 72 = 204 g."],"answer":"132.0 g"},"chem-11-09-b42":{"idea":"Rings and pi bonds add up to the degree of unsaturation.","steps":["DoU = (2 × 6 + 2 − 10) ÷ 2 = 2.","There is no ring, so both units are pi bonds.","That means one triple bond or two double bonds.","So it is an alkyne or a diene."],"others":"A cycloalkene has a ring, which is excluded here. An alkene has only one pi bond, so DoU would be 1. An alkane would have DoU 0.","answer":"An alkyne (one C-triple-bond-C) or a diene (two C=C), since DoU = 2 and no ring"},"chem-11-09-b43":{"idea":"Two reasons stop cyclooctatetraene from being aromatic.","steps":["It has 8 pi electrons, and 8 = 4n with n = 2, not 4n + 2.","A planar ring would be anti-aromatic and unstable.","So the molecule twists into a non-planar tub shape.","The tub breaks conjugation, so it behaves like separate alkenes."],"others":"It is not aromatic in experiments. Eight-membered rings are not banned in general; the issue is the electron count. n = 1.5 is not a whole number.","answer":"8 is 4n (n=2), not 4n+2, and the real molecule is a non-planar tub that breaks conjugation"},"chem-11-09-b44":{"idea":"When two groups compete, the stronger activating group wins.","steps":["–CH₃ activates the ring and directs ortho/para.","–NO₂ deactivates the ring, so its effect mainly slows the reaction.","The ring is most reactive at positions ortho and para to CH₃.","So the third group goes there."],"others":"The stronger deactivating group does not always decide. Substitution does still occur. The new group does not go midway between the two groups.","answer":"Mainly ortho/para to -CH3: the activating group controls where the new group goes"},"chem-11-09-b45":{"idea":"One product means a symmetric alkene.","steps":["Butan-2-one is CH₃–CO–CH₂CH₃.","Join two of these at the C=O carbons to replace C=O by C=C.","The alkene is CH₃CH₂–C(CH₃)=C(CH₃)–CH₂CH₃.","This is 3,4-dimethylhex-3-ene."],"others":"2-Methylbut-1-ene gives a ketone and formaldehyde. 3-Methylpent-2-ene gives a ketone and acetaldehyde. 2,3-Dimethylbut-2-ene gives only acetone.","answer":"3,4-dimethylhex-3-ene, C2H5C(CH3)=C(CH3)C2H5"},"chem-11-09-b46":{"idea":"Count the O atoms in the products.","steps":["Products: 6 CO₂ have 12 O. 3 H₂O have 3 O. Total 15 O atoms.","15 ÷ 2 = 7.5 mol O₂.","Check: x + y/4 = 6 + 6/4 = 7.5."],"answer":"7.5 mol"},"chem-11-09-b47":{"idea":"The alkyl cation can rearrange. The acylium ion cannot.","steps":["In alkylation, a primary or secondary cation can shift H or an alkyl group.","It becomes a more stable cation before attacking the ring.","This gives unexpected products.","The acylium ion is stabilised by resonance, so it does not rearrange."],"others":"The alkyl halide does not simply give an alcohol. AlCl₃ does not reduce the ring. Polyacylation is not common, since the acyl group deactivates the ring.","answer":"Carbocation rearrangement: the alkyl cation can shift H or alkyl first; the acylium ion does not"},"chem-11-09-b48":{"idea":"Markovnikov's rule is explained by the stability of the carbocation.","steps":["H⁺ attacks the double bond first.","It adds to the carbon that gives the more stable, more substituted carbocation.","X⁻ then attaches to that carbon.","This is why X ends up on the more substituted carbon."],"others":"The less stable cation forms more slowly, not faster. X⁻ does not add first. The addition is two-step, not one step.","answer":"H+ adds first to give the more stable (more substituted) carbocation, then X- adds to that carbon"},"chem-11-09-b49":{"idea":"A radical chain works only if both propagation steps release energy.","steps":["Step 1: X· adds to the alkene.","Step 2: the carbon radical takes H from H–X.","For HBr, both steps are exothermic.","For HCl, step 2 is uphill. For HI, step 1 is uphill."],"others":"Peroxide breaks into radicals by itself, whichever acid is present. Acid strength is not the key. Electronegativity of Br is not the reason either.","answer":"Only for HBr are both chain steps exothermic: with HCl, H-abstraction from the strong H-Cl bond is uphill; with HI, I-radical adding to the alkene is uphill"},"chem-11-09-b50":{"idea":"More s-character makes the carbon hold electrons more tightly and its H more acidic.","steps":["Alkyne C–H: carbon is sp, 50% s.","Alkene C–H: carbon is sp², 33% s.","Alkane C–H: carbon is sp³, 25% s.","So acidity falls: alkyne > alkene > alkane."],"others":"They are not equally acidic. The other two orders have the s-character trend reversed.","answer":"Alkyne C-H > alkene C-H > alkane C-H"}};
